Tara: opened a PR to add a short section on prefetch/speculative tokens to the 429 + timeout troubleshooting doc. includes a tiny Go SDK example for non-blocking fallback. PR: https://git.redwood.ai/redwood/docs/pull/1420
Miguel: interesting. prefetching is useful but risky for cost; can we include a decision sentence (prefetch only when latency critical and budget tolerates extra tokens)?
Tara: added that line. also included a mini decision row: Prefetch (low-latency, higher token cost) vs Conservative-replay (lower cost, higher tail latency).
Ben: can we show a Go snippet that resumes a stream and falls back to a short speculative request without duplicating tokens? sketch below:
```go
// pseudo-Go: resume stream + speculative short request on 429
prefix, _ := kv.Get(ctx, sessionKey)
stream, err := client.StreamGenerate(ctx, prompt, WithKVPreset(prefix))
if err == nil {
  for chunk := range stream.Chunks() {
    ui.Append(chunk.Text)
    prefix.LastIndex = chunk.LastIndex
  }
} else if errors.Is(err, ErrTooManyRequests) {
  // speculative short request: request a small window overlapping last emitted tokens
  replayStart := max(0, prefix.LastIndex-8)
  resp, _ := client.Generate(ctx, prompt, WithKVRange(replayStart, prefix.LastIndex), WithMaxTokens(56))
  // client-side dedupe: drop overlap
  ui.Append(dedupe(resp.Text, prefix.OverlapChars))
} else {
  return err
}
```
